> You do, of course, know what "real soon now" means to a programmer,
> don't you?

As in "vapourware"?

The actual phrase I got by email was "real soon now and we mean it" or
something like that. I'm reading this newsgroup very frequently
now, since it all sounded quite convincing, and I expect 3.1 to
pop up any minute....

I can't speak for the dev team (as I am not a member) but they
seem to have a two goals with 3.1:

- They want to surprise us with 3.1, and therefore refuse to give
  any information on what will be part of 3.1.
- They want a good quality release so 3.1 is tested and debugged
  extensively before it is made public.

The unfortunate result is that nothing comes out of them until
they are finished. This is a pity since a program like nethack
needs something like two or three new versions a year to keep
everybody's attention. Linux does the opposite; a new version of
kernel every week, and people are releasing software for it at
even higher rates. They need a special newsgroup to announce all
the new releases, and keeping up to date is near impossible. But
at least it is exciting!

But it's the dev teams choice of course, since they are doing the
programming.

